## 1. Executive Summary

This report details the development and validation of a comprehensive demand forecasting and inventory optimization pipeline for retail supply chains. Leveraging the Walmart Recruiting – Store Sales Forecasting dataset, the project follows the CRISP-DM (Cross-Industry Standard Process for Data Mining) methodology to derive a robust, data-driven (s, S) inventory policy under stochastic lead times.

The pipeline integrates Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA), probabilistic forecasting (Prophet vs. XGBoost), and Monte Carlo simulation. Prophet emerged as the superior forecasting model, achieving a Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) of 12.4%. Subsequently, a simulation-optimization framework evaluated 64 (s, S) policy combinations against stochastic demand and lead-time variability. The selected knee-point policy (s = 35,818; S = 40,317) achieves a 97.8% service level at an expected total cost of $83,377, significantly outperforming the deterministic Economic Order Quantity (EOQ) baseline by explicitly accounting for real-world uncertainty.

