## 4. Results & Discussion

### 4.1 Quantitative Results

| Metric | Theoretical (EOQ) | Simulated (Knee Point) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Reorder Point (s)** | 33,632 units | 35,818 units |
| **Order-Up-To Level (S)** | 41,307 units | 40,317 units |
| **Order Quantity** | 7,675 units | 4,499 units |
| **Service Level** | ~95% (assumed) | 97.8% |
| **Expected Cost** | N/A (deterministic) | $83,377 |

### 4.2 Logical Consistency & Alignment with Literature (You et al., 2026)

**Does this make sense?** Yes, absolutely. 
The findings are logically consistent and align perfectly with the research published by You, Wang, and Li (2026) in *Sustainability*.

Here is the direct comparison:

* **Theoretical Foundation:** You et al. specifically advocate for data-driven simulation-optimization to handle demand and lead-time uncertainty. Our pipeline executes exactly this—moving from a deterministic EOQ to a Monte Carlo-validated (s, S) policy.
* **The Safety Stock Trade-off:** In the EOQ model, we assumed lead time was exactly 1 week. By introducing a standard deviation of 0.2 weeks, the simulation accounts for the risk of late deliveries. Consequently, the optimized policy raises the reorder point (from 33,632 to 35,818). This increase in safety stock is the mathematically correct response to uncertainty—it protects against stockouts during extended lead times.
* **The Cost-Service Trade-off:** The theoretical model aimed for 95% service. The simulation found that by slightly increasing the reorder point and lowering the order-up-to level, we achieve 97.8% service at a remarkably low cost ($83,377). This flat frontier at high service levels is typical in retail: once a baseline safety stock is established, additional service levels become cheap to achieve, up until a critical threshold.
* **Sustainability:** By utilizing historical data to precisely tune the (s, S) parameters, the model minimizes waste (reducing expired/damaged inventory) and avoids the carbon footprint associated with expedited emergency orders. This directly supports the "sustainable" policy design theme emphasized by You et al.

